   "start_page": 458,
   "summary": "This section covers Japanese cabinet politics, civil service reform, government finance, commodity price regulations, and foreign trade during November 1939. It details Minseito party leader Chuji Machida's refusal to join Prime Minister Nobuyuki Abe's cabinet, opposition postponing the creation of a Trade Ministry, and bureaucratic resistance to modifying the 1899 civil service security act. Economic reviews highlight bond assimilation challenges, Bank of Japan note issuance exceeding legal limits, official price hikes for rice, tobacco, and silk textiles, and severe shortages of coal and electric power. Foreign trade figures show substantial export growth to the yen bloc, an Australian wool quota allocation, and persistent Japanese anxiety regarding the approaching January 1940 expiration of the 1911 commercial treaty with the United States.",
   "title": "Report on Political and Economic Conditions in Japan, November 1939",
   "to": "Department of State"
  },
  {
